---
title: Pre-interceptors
description: The stage that runs before the handler — rejecting, enriching, and rewriting the message.
---

A pre-interceptor runs before the handler and returns the message the next stage will see. It
is the stage for anything that decides whether the work should happen, or fixes up its input
first.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandPreInterceptor                    // every command
ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>          // one command, or one hierarchy
```

Queries and events have the same pair — `IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — and
they behave identically.

## Observing

The untyped form takes the base marker and returns it. Return the input unchanged when you are
only watching:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Rewriting

The typed form returns the typed message, so a rewrite needs no cast:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

What the next stage receives is what you returned. Chain two of them and the second sees the
first's output.

Returning a **derived** message is allowed, and is often the point — `TCommand` is the contract,
and anything assignable to it satisfies it:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command, _clock.UtcNow));
}
```

The handler still receives it as a `PlaceOrder`. A handler that wants the enriched shape should
declare the enriched type as its message instead.

{% aside type="note" %}
Earlier previews had `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` for exactly this. It was
removed once the one-parameter form started returning the typed message, because the second
argument then bought nothing. Migration is a deletion: drop the second type argument.
{% /aside %}

## Rejecting

To stop the dispatch, abort:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant(ITenantAccessor tenant) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("no tenant on this request");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

`Abort` throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, which the pipeline treats as a stop rather than a
failure: **the exception stage does not run, and neither does the final stage.** The signal
travels to the caller unchanged.

Throwing your own exception instead is a different statement — that says the dispatch *failed*,
and it runs the failure path. Use whichever you actually mean.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Because an abort skips the final stage, anything a pre-interceptor opened — a transaction, a
lease — will not be closed there. Put the check ahead of whatever opens the resource with a
higher `[Weight]`, or open it somewhere a `try`/`finally` covers.
{% /aside %}

## Passing information forward

The message is not the only channel. When a later stage needs something that does not belong in
the message, use the context:

```csharp
public sealed class Correlate : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("correlationId", Guid.NewGuid());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Ordering

Within the stage: `[Weight]` descending, direct registrations before covariant ones. Be explicit
whenever two interceptors genuinely depend on the order:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Validate  : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
```

## Typical uses

**Validation.** Reject early, before the handler touches anything:

```csharp
public sealed class ValidateOrders : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (command.Lines.Count == 0)
            context.Abort("an order needs at least one line");

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

**Authorization**, at a high weight so it runs first.

**Normalisation** — trimming, casing, defaulting — so every handler downstream sees clean input.

**Correlation and timing**, paired with a final interceptor that reads what this one set.

## Scope it narrowly

An interceptor written against `ICommand` runs for every command in the application, including
nested dispatches. Narrow it to a base type you own when the work is not genuinely universal —
you get the typed message as well, which is worth having on its own.
